.SH NAME
messq-auth-hash - hash a credential from stdin into the auth-file form


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmessq auth hash [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Read ONE credential from stdin and print the SHA-256 hex form an auth-file
line carries. Hashes cover the WHOLE presented string including the msq1_
prefix and the id — there is no second parse of a credential anywhere in
messq. Newline rule: exactly one trailing LF (and one CR if CRLF) is
stripped; everything else hashes byte-for-byte. That means \fBecho $cred\fR
(which appends a newline) hashes DIFFERENTLY from \fBprintf '%s' $cred\fR;
tests pin both digests so scripts can rely on either stated recipe.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for hash


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-a\fP, \fB--addr\fP="unix:///run/messq/messq.sock"
	daemon address (unix://, http://, https://)

.PP
\fB--color\fP="auto"
	colour: auto|always|never

.PP
\fB--full-ids\fP[=false]
	print full ULIDs instead of abbreviated ones

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="auto"
	output mode: auto|table|json|ndjson

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	suppress narration; data and errors unaffected

.PP
\fB--timeout\fP=30s
	per-request deadline (0 = none)

.PP
\fB--token-file\fP=""
	file holding the bearer token, mode 0600

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=0]
	narrate (-vv dumps each HTTP exchange and the resolved config)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  printf '%s' "$MESSQ_TOKEN" | messq auth hash
  messq auth hash < token-copy.txt   # one trailing newline tolerated
.EE


